.SH NAME
goblin-mosh-server \- server-side helper for mosh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B goblin-mosh-server
new
[\-s]
[\-v]
[\-i \fIIP\fP]
[\-p \fIPORT\fP[:\fIPORT2\fP]]
[\-c \fICOLORS\fP]
[\-R \fISPEC\fP]
[\-A]
[\-X]
[\-t \fIMS\fP]
[\-b \fIBPS\fP]
[\-\-fips\-crypto]
[\-\- command...]
.br
.B goblin-mosh-server relay
[\-\-bind=\fIIP\fP]
[\-\-port=\fIPORT[:PORT2]\fP]
[\-\-fips\-crypto]
\fITARGET_IP TARGET_PORT\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP is a helper program for the
.BR mosh(1)
remote terminal application.

\fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP binds to a high UDP port and chooses an encryption
key to protect the session. It prints both on standard output,
detaches from the terminal, and waits for the \fBgoblin-mosh-client\fP to
establish a connection. It will exit if no client has contacted
it within 60 seconds (ten minutes for negotiated UDP relay setup).

By default, \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP binds to a port between 60000 and
61000 and executes the user's login shell.

On platforms with \fButempter\fP, \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP maintains an entry
in the
.BR utmp(5)
file to indicate its process ID, whether the session is connected,
and the client's current IP address.

\fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP exits when the client terminates the connection.

.SH UDP RELAY
The \fBrelay\fP subcommand is normally launched by goblin-mosh's \-J option
over SSH. It binds an unprivileged UDP socket, generates an independent
authentication key, prints \fBMOSH RELAY 1 SUITE BIND_IP PORT KEY\fP, detaches,
and relays only authenticated packets to a single fixed numeric destination.
It does not receive the terminal session key or run a remote terminal.

\fB\-\-bind=IP\fP defaults to the incoming SSH interface from SSH_CONNECTION.
\fB\-\-port=PORT[:PORT2]\fP defaults to 60001:60999.
\fB\-\-fips-crypto\fP requires the OpenSSL FIPS provider for the outer layer.
\fB\-\-check\fP prints protocol/suite support and exits without opening sockets.
\fB\-\-foreground\fP keeps the helper attached for diagnostics.

\fB\-\-startup-timeout=SECONDS\fP defaults to 600 (maximum 3600).
\fB\-\-idle-timeout=SECONDS\fP defaults to 86400 (1800 through 604800;
foreground diagnostics also allow shorter values).
Only a newer authenticated client packet renews the lease or changes its
return address. An authenticated empty inner payload closes the relay.
No relay heartbeats or unbounded application queues are used.

The destination server consumes \fBMOSH_RELAY_HOPS\fP (1 through 4) during
setup and acknowledges it with \fBMOSH RELAY-MTU 1 N\fP. It reserves 24 bytes
per hop (36 in FIPS mode) below the conservative 1216-byte outer datagram MTU.
Use the wrapper to arrange consistent keys, mode and MTU at both ends.

.SH OPTIONS

The argument "new" must be first on the command line to use
command-line options.

.TP
.B \-s
bind to the local interface used for an incoming SSH connection, given
in the \fBSSH_CONNECTION\fP environment variable (for multihomed
hosts)

.TP
.B \-v
Print some debugging information even after detaching.  More instances
of this flag will result in more debugging information.

.TP
.B \-\-fips\-crypto
Use the \fBaes128-gcm-v1\fP wire format and require cryptographic operations
from a configured OpenSSL 3 FIPS provider. This must match the client mode.
The server reports the selected suite in its setup response so the wrapper can
fail closed before starting UDP transport.

.TP
.B \-i \fIIP\fP
IP address of the local interface to bind (for multihomed hosts)

.TP
.B \-p \fIPORT\fP[:\fIPORT2\fP]
UDP port number or port-range to bind.  \fB\-p 0\fP will let the
operating system pick an available UDP port.

.TP
.B \-c \fICOLORS\fP
Number of colors to advertise to applications through TERM (e.g. 8, 256)

.TP
.B \-l \fINAME=VALUE\fP
Locale-related environment variable to try as part of a fallback
environment, if the startup environment does not specify a character
set of UTF-8.

.TP
.B \-R \fISPEC\fP
Request a remote TCP forward. See
.BR goblin-mosh (1)
for the format.

.TP
.B \-A
Enable SSH agent forwarding for the login session, using the stream
forward supplied by the client.

.TP
.B \-X
Enable X11 forwarding for the login session, using the stream forward
supplied by the client. The login session receives
.B DISPLAY
and
.B XAUTHORITY
values for a temporary forwarded display.

.TP
.B \-t \fIMS\fP
Coalesce forwarded stream bytes for up to \fIMS\fP milliseconds before
sending.

.TP
.B \-b \fIBPS\fP
Cap forwarded stream payload bytes per second.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
These variables allow server-side configuration of Mosh's behavior.
They may be set by administrators in system login/rc files,
/etc/login.conf, or similar mechanisms, or users in their shell's
login/rc files.  \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP passes these variables to the login
session and shell that it starts, but changing them there will have no
effect.

.TP
.B MOSH_SERVER_NETWORK_TMOUT
If this variable is set to a positive integer number, it specifies how
long (in seconds) \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP will wait to receive an update from
the
client before exiting.  Since \fPmosh\fP is very useful for mobile
clients with intermittent operation and connectivity, we suggest
setting this variable to a high value, such as 604800 (one week) or
2592000 (30 days).  Otherwise, \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP will wait
indefinitely for a client to reappear.  This variable is somewhat
similar to the \fBTMOUT\fP variable found in many Bourne shells.
However, it is not a login-session inactivity timeout; it only applies
to network connectivity.

.TP
.B MOSH_SERVER_SIGNAL_TMOUT
If this variable is set to a positive integer number, it specifies how
long (in seconds) \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP will ignore SIGUSR1 while waiting
to receive an update from the client.  Otherwise, \fBSIGUSR1\fP will
always terminate \fBgoblin-mosh-server\fP.  Users and administrators may
implement scripts to clean up disconnected Mosh sessions.  With this
variable set, a user or administrator can issue

.nf
$ pkill -SIGUSR1 goblin-mosh-server
.fi

to kill disconnected sessions without killing connected login
sessions.

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_DELAY
Default forwarded-stream coalescing delay in milliseconds. See
.BR goblin-mosh (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_CLIENT_TERM
Client terminal type supplied by the \fBgoblin-mosh\fP wrapper. The server uses
this value as \fBTERM\fP for the login session. If it is unset or empty, the
server falls back to \fBxterm\fP or \fBxterm\-256color\fP based on the detected
color count.

.TP
.B MOSH_CLIENT_CAPS
Comma-separated capabilities advertised by the setup wrapper. When
\fBtmux-control-v1\fP is present, the server confirms it with
\fBMOSH TMUX control-v1\fP during bootstrap and forwards DCS-framed tmux -CC
output as reliable bytes. Only advertise this when the client supports it.

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_BANDWIDTH
Default forwarded-stream payload-byte-per-second cap. See
.BR goblin-mosh (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_STATE_ZSTD_DICT
Path to a zstd dictionary file to use for this server process. Files
produced by
.BR goblin-mosh-compile-dictionary (1)
are stored compressed with zstd level 22 and are decompressed before
use. The \fBmosh\fP wrapper sets this to a temporary uploaded copy
when \fB\-\-state\-zstd\-dict\fP is used.

.TP
.B MOSH_STATE_ZSTD_DICT_UNLINK
If set to 1, remove the file named by
.B MOSH_STATE_ZSTD_DICT
after reading it. The wrapper uses this so dictionaries are not cached
server-side.

.SH EXAMPLE

.nf
$ goblin-mosh-server

MOSH CONNECT 60001 UAkFedSsVJs2LfMeKyQB5g

goblin-mosh-server (mosh 1.1)
[...] (copyright notice omitted)

[goblin-mosh-server detached, pid = 20443]
.fi

